.TH SHAPEFUNCS 3 "2026-10-11" "fortran_shapefuncs 0.2.0" "Fortran Library Functions"
.SH NAME
shapefuncs \- finite element shape functions, including mapped infinite elements
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B use cubatures
.B use shapefuncs
.B type(shapefunc) :: s
.B s = shapefunc(q, order [, infin] [, family] [, nodes])
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Module
.B shapefuncs
provides the derived type
.BR shapefunc ,
the shape functions N_i of a finite element and their first and second
derivatives at the points of a
.B cubature
q from the module
.BR cubatures .
Values are real64.
.SS Elements
Lines, quadrilaterals and hexahedra of any order >= 0; triangles,
tetrahedra and wedges of any order >= 1, on the reference elements of
.BR cubatures ,
selected by
.BR q%elm .
Nodes are equispaced and numbered vertices first, then edges, faces and
interior, which gives the classical numbering for orders 1 to 3.
.SS Constants
.TP
.BR SHP_FIN ", " SHP_INF ", " SHP_CHP
Infinitude per direction: finite (default), infinite with mapping
functions for the geometry, infinite with chopped functions for the
solution. An infinite direction extends to infinity at xi = 1.
.TP
.BR SHP_LAGRANGE ", " SHP_SERENDIPITY
Element family: full Lagrange (default), or serendipity quadrilaterals and
hexahedra of order 1 to 3 (4, 8, 12 and 8, 20, 32 nodes).
.SS Constructor
.TP
.BR shapefunc ( q ", " order " [, " infin "] [, " family "] [, " nodes ])
.I infin
has size 1 or
.BR q%dim .
.I nodes
is a permutation: the caller's node k is node nodes(k) of the default
numbering, and every output follows the caller's numbering.
.SS Components
.BR elm ", " dim ", " order ", " family ", " nnodes ", " npoints ", " infin(3) ,
.BR lattice(:,nnodes) ", " coords(dim,nnodes) ,
.BR func(nnodes,npoints) ", " derv(dim,nnodes,npoints) ,
.BR curv(dim,dim,nnodes,npoints) .
.SS Type-bound procedures
.TP
.BR "call s%set" ( q ", " order " [, " infin "] [, " family "] [, " nodes ])
Build the shape functions in place, like the constructor.
.TP
.BR "call s%eval" ( xi ", " func ", " derv ", " curv )
Evaluate at any point xi of the reference element.
.TP
.BR s%is_valid ()
True if set and consistent.
.TP
.BR "call s%summary" "([" unit "]), " "call s%show" "([" unit "]), " "call s%numbering" "([" unit "])"
Write the element, order, family, infinitude and numbers of nodes and
points; show also writes every value and first derivative, numbering every
nodal coordinate.
.TP
.BR "call s%destroy" ()
Deallocate and reset.
.SH NOTES
Invalid arguments to the constructor or
.B set
stop the program with
.B error stop
and a message that starts with "shapefunc%set:". Errors cannot be caught.
.PP
Kinds are not exported; use real64 from iso_fortran_env. The module has no
state, and everything except the output procedures is pure.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
use cubatures
use shapefuncs
type(cubature) :: q
type(shapefunc) :: s
q = cubature(CUB_HEX, 4)
s = shapefunc(q, 2)                  ! 27-node hexahedron
print *, s%nnodes, sum(s%func(:,1))  ! 27  1.0000000000000000
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The complete reference is llms.txt in the source distribution and
https://willklausler.github.io/fortran_shapefuncs/
.SH AUTHOR
Will Klausler
